findings.body:
The head commit (09a3ae3) changes `crates/tauri-cli/metadata-v2.json` so that `"tauri"` goes from `2.8.3` back to `2.8.2`. The version step's own output had `2.8.3`. This PR also bumps `crates/tauri/Cargo.toml` to 2.8.3 and sets the schema `$id` to `.../config/2.8.3`, so the metadata now disagrees with the released crate.

This file is compiled into the CLI via `include_str!` in `crates/tauri-cli/src/init.rs` and `crates/tauri-cli/src/info/mod.rs`. Anything the CLI emits from `tauri` (scaffolded dependency versions, `tauri info` output) will report the previous release. `.scripts/ci/sync-cli-metadata.js` is the tool meant to write this field, and it would produce 2.8.3.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-cli/metadata-v2.json" lines="1-8"}

::callout{type="warning"}
Revert this one value to `2.8.3` so it matches `crates/tauri/Cargo.toml` and the schema `$id`.

The release bumps only the workspace crates, but `Cargo.lock` also changes the resolved dependency edges for third-party crates. `colored` 2.2.0 moves from `windows-sys 0.48.0` to `0.59.0`, `libloading` 0.8.6 moves from `windows-targets 0.48.5` to `0.52.6`, and `winapi-util` 0.1.9 moves from `windows-sys 0.48.0` to `0.59.0`. None of these crate versions changed, and no change file or changelog entry mentions them. A version-bump commit should not silently re-resolve unrelated dependencies, since that can change build output and transitive features for downstream users.

::diff{file="Cargo.lock" lines="1319-1324"}

I haven't confirmed why cargo re-resolved these edges (it may be a side effect of the covector step running a lock refresh). Run `cargo tree -i windows-sys@0.48.0` and `cargo tree -i windows-targets@0.48.5` on both base and head to see what still depends on the old versions, and decide whether these edges should be reverted or called out.
